Z||000004||StuGood||11-09-2001||12:57 PM||||On my 1986 SE V6, the temperature gage was kind of erratic.  As it turned out in my case, the needle wasn't well-stuck onto the the little pin it mounts to.<P>How I found out:<BR>Went to a local dealership, to purchase a new temp. sender, thinking that might be the problem.  While I was standing there at the parts coounter, asked the parts man if he knew a way to find out if the problem was the sender or the wiring or the gage.  He said, "Hmmmm...  lemme go ask!"<BR>  <BR>He returned with one of the dealership's mechanics.  The mechanic told me, "Pull the connector off the sending unit in the car, and turn the key on.  Ground one of (the 2) pins on the connector; the warning light on the dash should come on.  Ground the other pin, and the (temperature) gage should peg.  If both of those things happen, the gage and wiring in the car are good."<P>So I tried it.  Sure enough, when I grounded one pin, the warning light came on.  When I grounded the other, the gage pegged - and after I tried it a couple times, the needle fell off the pin!  There it lay, at the bottom of the gage.  The problem was obvious at that point.  I took apart the dash, heated the sender to a known temperature (the sender must be grounded, BTW-!), and reattached the needle to suit.<P>I still bought the sending unit, so I'd have a spare, plus I felt like they deserved at least that much business from me, for being so helpful.  Not to name names  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">, but they are the Pontiac dealership located on the west side of Wichita, KS  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Anyway, another long-winded Stu story  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> ; hope it helps you  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||3
